00:14:08.169 --> 00:14:11.927
The reason for this episode
was we got a question

00:14:11.927 --> 00:14:14.680
when we asked you guys to ask us anything,

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:19.540
a guy named John, @TheRealJohnDub
on Twitter asked us,

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:20.820
he said I'd love to hear you guys

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:22.343
discuss the trolley problem.

00:14:23.281 --> 00:14:25.660
Okay.
And so that got us,

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:27.550
gave us the idea what if
we did a whole episode

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:29.940
where we just went through some
different ethical conundrums

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:31.863
starting with what I think is,

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.283
most people have probably heard of this.

00:14:34.283 --> 00:14:36.500
They may not know what the specifics are

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.950
but you've heard of the trolley problem.

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:40.580
Maybe give a quick summary though.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:44.077
Yeah well I'm just gonna read it.

00:14:44.077 --> 00:14:48.601
You see a runaway trolley,
let's just say it's a train.

00:14:48.601 --> 00:14:50.390
Well no let's say you're in San Francisco.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:52.710
Well but I think the track, the tracks,

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:56.830
okay trolleys, the key here
is there's tracks involved.

00:14:56.830 --> 00:15:00.440
You see a runaway trolley
moving toward five tied up

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:04.793
or otherwise incapacitated
people lying on the tracks.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:10.330
You're standing next to
a lee-ver or a leh-ver,

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:13.010
whatever you prefer
that controls a switch.

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:16.110
If you pull the lever, the
trolley will be redirected

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:18.180
onto a side track and the five people

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.100
on the main track will be saved, however,

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:23.180
there is a single person
lying on the side track.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:26.360
You have two options, do
nothing and allow the trolley

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.100
to kill the five people on the main track,

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:30.940
or two, pull the lever,
diverting the trolley

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:33.420
onto the side track where
it will kill one person.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:35.680
Which is the more ethical option?

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:39.460
Now on its face, you may
have a knee-jerk reaction

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:42.360
and think that you know
what your answer would be,

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.540
but the reason that
this has been considered

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:49.307
a difficult ethical conundrum
or just an ethical conundrum

00:15:49.307 --> 00:15:54.307
is because there's an
active choice being made

00:15:54.448 --> 00:15:57.680
to kill someone that you
are responsible for--

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:59.467
Versus a passive choice just to sit there

00:15:59.467 --> 00:16:01.390
and let something happen--
Yeah in other words--

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:04.420
You weren't technically
involved, actively involved in.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:06.770
If you weren't present

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:09.609
and this situation just happened
in the universe somewhere,

00:16:09.609 --> 00:16:13.730
you wouldn't be morally culpable
for anything that happened

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.577
but because you're there
and now you have the option

00:16:15.577 --> 00:16:17.458
to pull the lever,

00:16:17.458 --> 00:16:20.640
that's what makes it
a difficult situation.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:24.463
Now, the trolley problem has been used

00:16:24.463 --> 00:16:28.270
or referenced quite a
bit as people are trying

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.970
to program autonomous vehicles.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:35.970
So self-driving cars are
being given basically programs

00:16:37.409 --> 00:16:39.690
to be able to--
Decide.

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:42.493
Make these kinds of
decisions and interestingly,

00:16:43.971 --> 00:16:48.270
most people, mosh
ethicists or most engineers

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:49.960
who looked at this have said
that the trolley problem

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:52.660
is not a great example of something

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:55.620
that an autonomous
vehicle needs to navigate

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:57.990
because it's so binary.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.049
Basically autonomous vehicles
do not enter situations

00:17:01.049 --> 00:17:03.760
where it's like one person or five people,

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:05.190
that's very uncommon.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:08.480
More often there is a
range of possibilities

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:11.127
and even within those
binary possibilities,

00:17:11.127 --> 00:17:13.402
there's a bunch of different
outcomes that could happen

00:17:13.402 --> 00:17:15.570
and so it's actually way more complex

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:18.320
than just giving it some
trolley problem program

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:21.380
or however you would go
about programming the car.

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:23.346
But having said all that,

00:17:23.346 --> 00:17:26.010
let's just say this is the situation.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:27.500
Now typically this doesn't happen.

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:29.330
People don't get tied to railroad tracks

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:31.173
outside of cartoons usually.

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:33.950
Or old westerns but let's just say--

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:36.341
I didn't think masseuses
used latex gloves either.

00:17:36.341 --> 00:17:37.890
That's right.

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:39.910
So let's just say you're
in this situation,

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:41.210
what would you do?

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.820
I feel like I would pull the
lever because I feel like

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:48.820
not doing anything is a, that's
still an act of omission.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:53.790
Well the first thing
that would happen to me

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.210
if I was in this situation,
I would be like oh,

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:58.898
crap, I am literally in the
